      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 4068.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 748,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Unique States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water at room temperature (found in rivers, oceans, rain).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F) (found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the air (found in humidity, clouds, steam).\n\n### 2. Why Water is Magical (Unique Scientific Properties)\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transport medium, carrying vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes (through our bloodstreams, through plant stems, and through the soil).\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Almost all liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different. When it freezes into ice, it actually *expands* and becomes **less dense**, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all marine life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a massive amount of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite Earth being covered in water, roughly **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nWater is the fundamental building block of life as we know it. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water. Without water, human cells cannot maintain their structure, chemical reactions cannot take place, and we would die within a few days.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Water shapes landscapes through erosion, drives weather patterns, and provides habitats for millions of aquatic species.\n\n**In short:** Water is the lifeblood of planet Earth—a simple molecule with complex properties that makes existence, weather, biology, and chemistry possible."
